\section{Compare Target Time Under a Fixed Cost Assumption}

This illustrative experiment is not VADL syntax or a result from the paper. Take a fixed program, an area budget of \(130\) arbitrary units, and estimated clock periods at the same synthesis conditions. The clock period estimates are assumed achievable and the workload runs at a fixed clock. \Cref{fig:visual-336} compares three hypothetical outcomes.

\begin{verbatim}
baseline = run_reference(binary)
for design in [A, B, C]:
    run = run_cycle_model(design, binary)
    require committed_effects(run) == baseline
    period, area = map_design(design, same_constraints)
    if area <= 130:
        record(design, run.cycles * period)    # target time, not host time
\end{verbatim}

Design A takes 1000 cycles at \(2.0\) ns and costs 100 area units, for \(2000\) ns. B takes 800 cycles at \(2.8\) ns and costs 115 units, for \(2240\) ns. A wins between those two, despite B's cycle reduction. C takes 800 cycles at \(2.1\) ns but costs 145 units; ignore its \(1680\) ns for this budget. The invariant is that only behaviorally matching runs under common assumptions enter this ranking; area is a feasibility check, not an unreported preference. A different workload or budget can reverse the recommendation.

For a measured rather than illustrative comparison, repeat host runs and report the aggregation rule, but do not average unlike target configurations. Preserve the exact generated sources and tool options as well as the high-level parameters: a changed synthesis constraint or memory latency is a design input, not measurement noise.

